# Implement the explicit remote service

## Goal

Make the same application service available remotely only when the operator has
explicitly chosen and safely configured a network deployment.

## Required work

- Define the remote MCP transport and lifecycle without changing mail semantics.
- Require authenticated clients, TLS termination, account and tenant isolation,
  rate limits, request bounds, audit-safe diagnostics, and revocation.
- Define secure session provisioning for a remote host without copying plaintext
  browser state or accepting account credentials in tool calls.
- Refuse wildcard or public binding unless the complete security configuration
  is present and validated.
- Publish one reproducible cloud deployment recipe and one local loopback
  recipe.

## Acceptance criteria

- Remote mode cannot start with anonymous access, plaintext transport, a shared
  cross-tenant secret namespace, or accidental public binding.
- Local stdio remains fully usable without remote mode or a cloud account.
- Remote and local transports pass the same contract suite.
- Revoking a remote client or Proton session stops subsequent authority without
  requiring a rebuild or data loss.
- The cloud recipe is deployed and exercised end to end from a fresh
  environment.

## Evidence

- Remote transport conformance tests
- Tenant-isolation, authentication, rate-limit, and revocation tests
- Redacted fresh-deployment transcript
